---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../components/Math.astro';
import { site, personNode } from '../data/site';

const softwareSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'SMVac',
  description:
    'A reproducible computational investigation of the tree-level O(4) bounce action of the RG-improved Standard Model potential: a validated numerical reference (Chebyshev spectral collocation) used to test established analytical approximations, with phase diagrams of the Higgs–top mass plane.',
  codeRepository: site.smvac,
  author: personNode,
  programmingLanguage: ['C++', 'Python'],
  version: '2.0.0-dev',
  dateModified: '2026-11-11',
};
---
<Base
  title="SMVac · electroweak vacuum stability"
  description="SMVac solves the tree-level bounce of the RG-improved Standard Model potential numerically, with a validated uncertainty, and uses it to test the analytical approximations to the vacuum decay action, with phase diagrams in the (Mh, Mt) plane."
  jsonLd={[softwareSchema]}

  ogImage="/og/smvac.png">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research software · C++ / Python</p>
      <h1>SMVac</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 42rem;">
        A numerical study of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model: the analytical
        approximations to the bounce action, tested against a validated numerical solution.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        <strong style="color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600;">Naman Goyal</strong> · May 2024 – May 2026,
        mentored by Prof. Rick Sandeepan Gupta (TIFR) · Version 2, November 2026 ·
        <a href={site.smvac} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
      <h2 id="overview-heading" style="margin-top: 1rem;"><span class="secno">01</span> Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The Standard Model of particle physics appears, from its measured parameters, to live on a
        knife's edge. At field values far beyond any collider, the effective Higgs potential turns
        over: the quartic coupling <Math tex={'\\lambda(\\mu)'} />, run up in energy, becomes negative
        at scales of order <Math tex={'10^{10}\\text{–}10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. The electroweak
        vacuum in which we live is then not the absolute minimum of the theory but a
        <em>metastable</em> one, destined in principle to decay by quantum tunneling. Whether that
        decay can ever matter is controlled by the semiclassical bounce action <Math tex={'S'} />:
        the vacuum survives cosmological timescales provided <Math tex={'S'} /> is large enough. The
        measured Higgs and top masses place the Standard Model close to the boundary between
        absolute stability and metastability.
      </p>
      <p>
        SMVac solves the tree-level O(4) bounce of the RG-improved potential
        <Math tex={'V = \\lambda_P(\\phi)\\,\\phi^4/4'} /> numerically, with a validated uncertainty,
        and uses that solution as a benchmark for the closed-form approximations in the literature:
        across the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane and along lines approaching the
        absolute-stability boundary. It studies the tree-level action only. It does not reproduce or
        improve the complete calculation of Buttazzo et al. (2013), which uses the one-loop action,
        a two-loop <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\rm eff}'} /> and the field anomalous dimension.
      </p>
      <p>
        Version 2 replaces the original implementation. An audit showed that the quantity the
        original code reported, an “RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov action”, is a first-order
        perturbative estimate about the Fubini–Lipatov instanton that is unbounded below, and that
        its reported deviations from the Fubini–Lipatov formula came from an undocumented
        <Math tex={'\\phi^6/M_{\\rm Pl}^2'} /> term and from Planck-scale artefacts. Earlier
        versions of this page described that construction. The original code and data are
        preserved unchanged in the repository for traceability.
      </p>

      <dl class="findings">
        <div>
          <dt>Computed</dt>
          <dd>
            The tree-level bounce action over the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane, with a
            numerical uncertainty of <Math tex={'10^{-12}'} /> or better at standard-tier points.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Tested</dt>
          <dd>
            The closed forms. <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL} = 8\\pi^2/(3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} /> is a
            rigorous lower bound, low by 0.03–0.17% where <Math tex={'S < 5000'} /> and by up to
            34–50% near the stability boundary. Espinosa's first-order formula stays within 1.54% at
            all 21,626 standard-tier points of the controlled scans.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Not computed</dt>
          <dd>
            The one-loop decay rate. All statements hold within the tree-level RG-improved scheme;
            they are not corrections to published lifetimes.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading"><span class="secno">02</span> The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Why the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane? The top quark drives <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> down
        through the RG flow, the Higgs mass pushes it up; the stability verdict is therefore a curve
        in the Higgs–top mass plane, and the measured point,
        <Math tex={'M_h = 125.13 \\pm 0.11\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> and
        <Math tex={'M_t = 172.60 \\pm 0.27\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> (PDG 2026), sits close to it. Classic
        analyses (Buttazzo et al. 2013) mapped this boundary with multi-loop precision.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard estimate of the vacuum lifetime uses the action of the scale-invariant
        Fubini–Lipatov instanton, <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL} = 8\\pi^2/(3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} />,
        evaluated at the minimum of the running coupling (Buttazzo et al. 2013, eq. 81). This is
        exact only for a constant coupling. The running of <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> breaks scale
        invariance, and near the stability boundary the potential develops a second, nearly
        degenerate minimum below <Math tex={'M_{\\rm Pl}'} />. The leading correction from the
        running was derived by Espinosa (2020). SMVac measures numerically how large the error of
        the leading formula is and where it matters, how well Espinosa's correction and other
        closed forms capture it, and where all of them fail.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
      <h2 id="method-heading"><span class="secno">03</span> Method</h2>
      <p>The problem, as defined and implemented in the repository:</p>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>Input <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> and <Math tex={'\\alpha_s(M_Z)'} /></li>
        <li>NNLO matching at <Math tex={'\\mu = M_t'} /> (Buttazzo et al. 2013, linear fits)</li>
        <li>3-loop RG evolution (4-loop QCD) to <Math tex={'M_{\\rm Pl}'} />, frozen above</li>
        <li>RG-improved quartic <Math tex={'\\lambda_P(\\phi)'} /> at <Math tex={'\\mu = \\phi'} /></li>
        <li>Tree-level O(4) bounce, solved numerically</li>
        <li>Decay probability <Math tex={'\\wp'} /> in our past light cone</li>
        <li>Classify: stable, metastable (<Math tex={'\\wp < 1'} />), unstable</li>
        <li>Repeat over an <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> grid → phase diagram</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Effective potential</h3>
      <p>
        The potential is the tree-level form with the running quartic evaluated at
        <Math tex={'\\mu = \\phi'} />, in one of two prescriptions: the one-loop Coleman–Weinberg
        <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\rm eff}'} /> (<code>cw1</code>) or the
        <Math tex={'\\overline{\\rm MS}'} /> coupling <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> (<code>tree</code>):
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'V(\\phi) \\;=\\; \\tfrac{1}{4}\\,\\lambda_P(\\phi)\\,\\phi^{4}, \\qquad \\lambda_P \\in \\bigl\\{\\lambda_{\\rm eff}^{\\,\\rm 1\\text{-}loop},\\ \\lambda_{\\overline{\\rm MS}}\\bigr\\}, \\qquad \\mu = \\phi.'}
      />
      <p>
        Non-SM operators such as <Math tex={'\\phi^6/M_{\\rm Pl}^2'} /> are not part of the problem:
        they exist only as an explicit, flagged option and never enter Standard Model results.
      </p>

      <h3>Renormalization group evolution</h3>
      <p>
        The couplings at <Math tex={'\\mu = M_t'} /> come from the NNLO fits of Buttazzo et al.
        (2013), eqs. 55–60. The gauge couplings, <Math tex={'y_t'} /> and <Math tex={'\\lambda'} />
        run at three loops, <Math tex={'y_b'} /> and <Math tex={'y_\\tau'} /> at two, with four-loop
        QCD, integrated by classical RK4 in <Math tex={'t = \\ln\\mu^2'} /> up to
        <Math tex={'M_{\\rm Pl} = 1.22 \\times 10^{19}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, above which the couplings
        are frozen. An independent Python implementation (DOP853) cross-checks the running.
      </p>
      <Math block tex={'\\frac{d g_i}{d \\ln \\mu} \\;=\\; \\beta_{g_i}^{\\rm (3\\text{-}loop)}.'} />

      <h3>Bounce equation and decay probability</h3>
      <p>
        With <Math tex={'\\tau = \\ln r'} />, <Math tex={'f = r\\phi'} /> and
        <Math tex={'x = \\ln\\phi'} />, the O(4) bounce equation
        <Math tex={"\\phi'' + 3\\phi'/r = V'(\\phi)"} /> becomes
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={"f'' = f + f^{3}\\,G(x), \\qquad G = \\lambda + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{d\\lambda}{dx}, \\qquad x = \\ln f - \\tau,"}
      />
      <p>
        with <Math tex={'f \\sim e^{\\pm\\tau}'} /> at the two ends. Every solution satisfies two exact
        identities, Derrick's and the dilatation identity, which are monitored at every point:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={"S = 2\\pi^{2}\\!\\int\\! d\\tau\\,\\Bigl[\\tfrac{1}{2}(f'-f)^{2} + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\lambda f^{4}\\Bigr] = T + V, \\qquad T + 2V = 0, \\qquad \\int \\frac{d\\lambda}{dx}\\, f^{4}\\, d\\tau = 0."}
      />
      <p>
        The reference solver was chosen by comparing five methods: the legacy construction,
        shooting in two formulations, adaptive collocation, and Chebyshev spectral collocation. It
        is Chebyshev–Lobatto collocation on <Math tex={'[\\tau_c - 15, \\tau_c + 15]'} /> with Robin
        conditions <Math tex={"f' = \\pm f"} /> and damped Newton iteration, solved at 256 and 384
        nodes and refined if the identities do not hold to <Math tex={'10^{-9}'} />. The uncertainty
        is the change between the two resolutions plus the identity residuals. Where collocation
        fails, two independent shooting solvers re-solve the point, and a result is accepted only
        if they agree to <Math tex={'10^{-8}'} />.
      </p>
      <p>
        The decay probability in our past light cone follows Buttazzo et al. (2013), eqs. 80–82,
        with the bounce scale set by the bounce radius <Math tex={'R'} />:
      </p>
      <Math block tex={'\\wp \\;=\\; 0.15\\,\\frac{\\Lambda_B^{4}}{H_0^{4}}\\,e^{-S}, \\qquad \\Lambda_B = 1/R.'} />
      <p>
        A point is <em>stable</em> if <Math tex={'\\min\\lambda_P \\ge 0'} />, <em>metastable</em> if
        <Math tex={'\\wp < 1'} />, and <em>unstable</em> otherwise.
      </p>

      <h3>Analytical approximations under test</h3>
      <p>
        The parameter <Math tex={"q = |\\lambda_{\\min}|/\\lambda''_{\\min}"} />, with
        <Math tex={"\\lambda'' = d^2\\lambda/d(\\ln\\phi)^2"} /> at the minimum, measures the departure
        from scale invariance: large <Math tex={'q'} /> is the thick-wall regime, small
        <Math tex={'q'} /> the thin-wall regime near the absolute-stability boundary.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL} = 8\\pi^2/(3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} /> (Fubini; Lipatov; Buttazzo et al.
          eq. 81): a rigorous lower bound on every finite-action bounce, by the method of Wipf (1985).
        </li>
        <li>
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm E1} = S_{\\rm FL}\\,(1 + \\tfrac{13}{72q})'} /> (Espinosa 2020, eq. 3.21):
          first order in <Math tex={'1/q'} />, an approximation and not a bound;
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm E2}'} /> adds the second-order term (Espinosa eq. 6.5).
        </li>
        <li>
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm curv} = S_{\\rm FL}/(1 - \\tfrac{13}{72q})'} />: the resummed first-order
          form, which diverges at <Math tex={'q = 13/72'} />.
        </li>
        <li>
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm CGM}'} />: a variational upper estimate over the Fubini–Lipatov trial
          shape, rigorous only if the Coleman–Glaser–Martin infimum is attained;
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm CGM2}'} /> uses a two-parameter trial family.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These analytical results are prior work, not SMVac results: the lower-bound method is due to
        Wipf (1985), the thick-wall expansion, including the coefficient <Math tex={'13/72'} /> and
        the second-order term, to Espinosa (2020), and earlier numerical solutions of the same
        tree-level problem exist at the central SM point (Di Luzio, Isidori &amp; Ridolfi 2016).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="results-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">04</span> Results</h2>
        <p>
          Two scans map the plane with the numerical bounce (prescription <code>cw1</code>): the full
          <Math tex={'[0, 250]^2\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> plane on a <Math tex={'0.5\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />
          grid (250,500 points) and a close-up
          <Math tex={'[110, 140] \\times [160, 185]\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> on a
          <Math tex={'0.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> grid (301,101 points). Collocation failures are
          re-solved by shooting or, where the bound alone decides, classified by the rigorous action
          bound; 10 of 5,187 remain unresolved. Controlled statements are restricted to
          <Math tex={'[120, 130] \\times [166, 182]\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, where the NNLO matching fits
          apply; elsewhere the matching is extrapolated, so those areas illustrate the model and are
          not physical predictions.
        </p>
        <p>
          At <Math tex={'M_h = 125.2\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> the absolute-stability boundary
          <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\min} = 0'} /> lies at <Math tex={'M_t = 171.144\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />
          and the metastability boundary <Math tex={'\\wp = 1'} /> at
          <Math tex={'M_t = 178.604\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. The PDG 2026 masses lie entirely in the
          metastable region: all 229 close-up grid points inside their joint
          <Math tex={'2\\sigma'} /> region are metastable, with <Math tex={'S = 1915\\text{–}5003'} />,
          and at the central values <Math tex={'S = 2778'} />, <Math tex={'\\wp = 10^{-971}'} />, with
          the instability scale at <Math tex={'\\Lambda_I = 10^{11.5}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. These are
          statements of the tree-level scheme, without the uncertainty of
          <Math tex={'\\alpha_s'} />, and they do not change the established picture of a metastable
          Standard Model vacuum.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/phase_diagram_full_contours.png"
        alt="Phase diagram of the electroweak vacuum over the Higgs–top mass plane from 0 to 250 GeV, from the numerical bounce. Green marks stability, yellow metastability, red instability, dark grey the non-perturbative region and light grey the Planck-scale dominated region without a finite bounce. Blue contours of constant action run through the metastable and unstable regions, and an inset magnifies the PDG 2026 point with its 1σ and 2σ regions, inside the metastable band just above the stability boundary."
        width={2147}
        height={1501}
        label="Fig. 1"
        caption="The electroweak vacuum over the (M_h, M_t) plane from the numerical bounce (prescription cw1, 0.5 GeV grid), with contours of constant tree-level action S, the boundaries λ_min = 0 and ℘ = 1, and the PDG 2026 masses with their joint 1σ and 2σ regions (inset: 0.05 GeV grid). Outside the controlled region [120, 130] × [166, 182] GeV the matching is extrapolated, so those areas illustrate the model and are not physical predictions. Figure from SMVac."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/phase_diagram_closeup_contours.png"
        alt="Close-up of the phase diagram for Higgs masses from 110 to 140 GeV and top masses from 160 to 185 GeV on a 0.05 GeV grid: a yellow metastable band between the red unstable region above and the green stable region below, contours of constant action from S = 300 to 20,000, a light grey Planck-scale dominated wedge at large Higgs mass, and the PDG 2026 point with its 1σ and 2σ ellipses inside the metastable band."
        width={2162}
        height={1471}
        label="Fig. 2"
        caption="Close-up [110, 140] × [160, 185] GeV on a 0.05 GeV grid, with contours of constant S. It gives the same class as the full plane at all 3,111 shared grid points. Of the 4,433 collocation failures, the shooting fallback decides 4,417 and the action bound 7; nine isolated cells on the edge of the Planck-scale dominated region remain unresolved. Figure from SMVac."
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Former anchors of the next section; kept so existing links still land on it. -->
    <span id="analytical-vs-rg-improved" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span id="conformal-vs-trial" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <section class="section" id="approximations" aria-labelledby="approx-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="approx-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">05</span> Analytical approximations vs. the numerical bounce</h2>
        <p>
          The leading formula <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL}'} /> is a rigorous lower bound and holds at every
          scan point. Where <Math tex={'S < 5000'} />, the bulk of the metastable and unstable region,
          it underestimates the action by <Math tex={'3 \\times 10^{-4}'} /> to
          <Math tex={'1.7 \\times 10^{-3}'} />, following the first-order law of Espinosa (2020),
          <Math tex={'(S - S_{\\rm FL})/S_{\\rm FL} \\to 13/(72q)'} />, as the wall thickens. It
          degrades toward the absolute-stability boundary, to at most 34% (<code>cw1</code>) and 50%
          (<code>tree</code>), where <Math tex={'|\\lambda_{\\min}| \\to 0'} />, a second minimum
          becomes nearly degenerate and the bounce becomes thin-walled.
        </p>
        <p>
          Among the closed forms, the first-order formula <Math tex={'S_{\\rm E1}'} /> is the most
          accurate near criticality: its maximum error over all 21,626 standard-tier points of the
          controlled scans is 1.54%, with median <Math tex={'9 \\times 10^{-6}'} />. For
          <Math tex={'q \\lesssim 1'} /> this lies outside its formal domain, so the accuracy is
          empirical; it degrades to −15% at <Math tex={'q = 0.044'} /> on a Standard Model line
          approaching the boundary. The variational estimate <Math tex={'S_{\\rm CGM}'} /> has the
          smallest typical error in the bulk (median <Math tex={'2 \\times 10^{-7}'} />), but it and
          the resummed <Math tex={'S_{\\rm curv}'} /> diverge near <Math tex={'q = 13/72'} />, and a
          two-parameter trial family moves the divergence to <Math tex={'q = 3/32'} /> without
          removing it. No estimate is uniformly best.
        </p>
        <p>
          The divergence has an elementary explanation. For <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> quadratic in
          <Math tex={'\\ln\\phi'} />, a trial of fixed shape <Math tex={'g'} /> gives a finite estimate
          if and only if <Math tex={'q > \\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{Var}(\\ln g)'} />, which equals
          <Math tex={'13/72'} /> for the Fubini–Lipatov shape. The exact bounce escapes the condition
          by forming a flat core and a thin wall with a much weaker tail. The repository states this
          as an elementary lemma and claims no novelty for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the decay criterion these errors barely matter. Near the metastability boundary
          (<Math tex={'450 < S < 650'} />) the error of <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL}'} /> is 0.14–0.25
          units of action, which moves the <Math tex={'\\wp = 1'} /> boundary by
          <Math tex={'0.0025\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> in <Math tex={'M_t'} />. The ambiguities of RG
          improvement move it much further: by <Math tex={'0.094\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> through the
          identification of the bounce scale, and by <Math tex={'0.12\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> between
          <code>cw1</code> and <code>tree</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/analytic_error_scaling.png"
        alt="Log–log plot of the relative deviation of four analytical estimates from the numerical bounce action against the conformal-breaking parameter q, for the controlled-scan points. The Fubini–Lipatov bound follows the dashed thick-wall law 13/(72q), from about 0.5 at small q down to 3 × 10⁻⁴ at large q; the first-order, resummed and variational estimates lie orders of magnitude lower at large q and rise steeply at small q. Stars mark parabolic-model benchmarks."
        width={1232}
        height={916}
        label="Fig. 3"
        caption="Error of the analytical estimates against the conformal-breaking parameter q, for the standard-tier points of both controlled scans (circles cw1, squares tree): S_FL, S_E1 (first order, Espinosa 2020), S_curv and S_CGM. Dashed: the thick-wall law 13/(72q) for S_FL (Espinosa 2020). Stars: parabolic-model benchmarks. The dips in the S_E1 and S_curv errors are sign changes of S_est − S_num; neither is a bound. Figure from SMVac."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/near_critical.png"
        alt="Three panels on the approach to the absolute-stability boundary. (a) The excess of the numerical action over the Fubini–Lipatov bound against q: the parabolic model and four Standard Model lines fall on one curve. (b) Relative errors of five closed-form estimates against q: only the first-order formula stays within a few per cent down to q near 0.1, while the variational estimates diverge at q = 13/72 and q = 3/32. (c) The bounce action along the Standard Model lines against the distance δ from the critical top mass, rising from about 10³ to 10⁸."
        width={2647}
        height={923}
        label="Fig. 4"
        caption="Approach to the absolute-stability boundary. (a) S_num/S_FL − 1 against q for the parabolic model, the four SM lines and all controlled-scan points: the SM follows the one-parameter model curve to 2–3%. (b) Relative errors of S_FL, S_E1, S_curv, S_CGM and S_CGM2; only S_E1 stays finite and within a few per cent down to q ≈ 0.1, an accuracy that is empirical. Vertical lines mark the finiteness thresholds q = 13/72 and q = 3/32. (c) Action along the SM lines against δ = M_t − M_t^crit, with S_FL dashed. Figure from SMVac."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="validation" aria-labelledby="validation-heading">
      <h2 id="validation-heading"><span class="secno">06</span> Validation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>RG running.</strong> The couplings at <Math tex={'M_{\\rm Pl}'} /> reproduce Buttazzo
          et al. (2013), eq. 61, to its quoted precision, and the C++ (RK4) and Python (DOP853)
          implementations agree to <Math tex={'10^{-10}'} /> in <Math tex={'\\lambda(M_{\\rm Pl})'} />.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>β-functions.</strong> A literature-independent loop-order power-counting test,
          which also catches the transcription errors of the original code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bounce solver.</strong> It reproduces the Fubini–Lipatov action for constant
          <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> to <Math tex={'10^{-10}'} /> and agrees with the independent
          shooting solvers to <Math tex={'\\lesssim 10^{-11}'} /> on thick-wall, thin-wall and SM
          benchmarks. Checks that do not rely on that agreement: exponential self-convergence,
          long-double re-solution, domain size, quadrature-rule independence, perturbations of the
          potential coefficients, and the exact identities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Uncertainty.</strong> About <Math tex={'10^{-14}'} /> relative at generic points and
          <Math tex={'10^{-12}'} /> near criticality, with a per-point estimate below
          <Math tex={'1.1 \\times 10^{-9}'} /> in the standard tier. Numerical, approximation,
          parametric, theoretical and Planck-model uncertainties are kept separate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bounds.</strong> No solved point violates
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm FL} \\le S_{\\rm num} \\le S_{\\rm CGM}'} />.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tests and reproducibility.</strong> Seven C++ test suites (CTest) and Python tests.
          A seeded, stratified sample of 233 stored results, recomputed from a clean build, matched
          the stored values.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review.</strong> The repository records the audit of the original implementation,
          the comparison of numerical methods, a validation report and a referee-style scientific
          review, with the corrections that followed them.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <h2 id="limitations-heading"><span class="secno">07</span> Limitations</h2>
      <p>Stated plainly, because they bound what the results mean:</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem;">
          The full list is in the repository's README; the most important items:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Tree-level action only.</strong> The one-loop fluctuation determinant is not
            computed. Its effect on the decay exponent, and the prescription dependence it would
            cancel, are larger than the approximation errors quantified here.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a full lifetime calculation.</strong> Statements about the errors of the
            literature formula are internal to the tree-level RG-improved scheme. They are not
            corrections to published lifetimes (Buttazzo et al. 2013; Chigusa, Moroi &amp; Shoji 2017;
            Andreassen, Frost &amp; Schwartz 2018).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Local matching inputs.</strong> The linear NNLO fits restrict controlled statements
            to <Math tex={'[120, 130] \\times [166, 182]\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />; the rest of the plane is
            an extrapolation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Simplified <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\rm eff}'} />.</strong> No two-loop terms and no
            field anomalous dimension, so absolute-stability boundaries differ slightly from Buttazzo
            et al. (2013), eq. 64.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Planck scale.</strong> Bounces that reach <Math tex={'\\phi \\gtrsim M_{\\rm Pl}'} />,
            and the points with no finite bounce, depend on the arbitrary freezing of the couplings
            above <Math tex={'M_{\\rm Pl}'} />; they are flagged. Gravitational corrections are not
            included.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Deep thin wall.</strong> For <Math tex={'q \\lesssim 0.05'} /> the solvers do not
            converge, and only the rigorous lower bound is available.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-heading">
      <h2 id="code-heading"><span class="secno">08</span> Code</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={site.smvac} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SMVac</a> (version 2.0.0-dev) is
        a CMake C++14 library (matching, β-functions, RG evolution, potential, bounce, decay and
        stability, with Chebyshev, Brent and quadrature numerics) with command-line apps for a single
        point (JSON report) and for grid scans (CSV with a metadata header), seven C++ test suites,
        and an independent Python reference implementation with its own tests. Scans are
        config-driven and parallel, and every figure is regenerated from data. The original
        implementation is preserved unchanged in <code>legacy/</code>.
      </p>
